== Tracing DSLs

When AI became a core focus of the tech industry, the focus of compiler and programming
langauge authors changed to suit the needs of programmers targetting specialized
AI hardware.

AI kernel development involves generating of lots of code specialized for specific
hardware and inputs and typical GPU programming models can make this difficult.
Prior to the popularization of tracing JITs, state of the art GPU programs were often written
using a CUDA C++ template library called CUTLASS #citen(<nvidia_cutlass_2023>),
which can have poor compile times and be difficult to use but can generate good
performance from relatively high level information
#footnote[
Michael Goldfarb of NVIDIA summarized the tradeoff as follows
@Goldfarb2025CuTeDSLJAX:
- C++ templates are slow to compile
- C++ templates are inconvenient
- DL community has fully embraced Python
This is not to say CUDA C++ is not used anymore, but for many developers,
Python kernel authoring frameworks provide better tradeoffs.
].

The two-stage process of writing programs that _generate_ kernels and then
executing those generated programs lends itself well to metaprogramming.
Kernel authors also typically care about different things at the kernel
generation (or metaprogramming) stage and the kernel execution stage.
When they write meta programs, they typically use Python and want regular
Python features available to them (like classes, polymorphism, higher-ordered programming),
but the generated programs do not need these features.
This set of needs led to an explosion of programming models that provide different
feature sets for different _stages_ of programming.

// While not the first tracing DSL, Tensorflow's DSL #citen(<agrawal2019tensorflow>)
// is a useful example because we have a well-established corpus of open-source Tensorflow
// code both with and without the tracing DSL.
