=== Staged Programming

Staged programming involves several _stages_ where only _parts_ of the user's program
are evaluated.
A single-stage program's evaluation semantics are straightforward; the user simply executes the program.
The simplest multi-stage programming model has two stages: first, the
user's program is executed (or _traced_) and another program is
#emph[generated by that program as a by product],
and in the second stage, the generated program is executed.
This is useful when developing GPU programs because the stages do not
need to evaluate in the same way.
The user's program may first execute regular Python code on the CPU, and the
generated program may be a GPU kernel that needs to be executed with a call
to a driver library.

This model is distinct from macro programming in Lisp #xref(<sec:lisp>) because Lisp
macros are programs that take programs and return new programs, but 

Most popular modern traced programming models are built in Python, so that is
what we will use for examples in this section.
These programming models typically provide a Python decorator that handles the
staging infrastructure.
In the first stage, the user's program is interpreted by the Python interpreter,
and the arguments to functions and GPU kernels are _symbolic_.
Operations performed on runtime values in the first stage operate on symbols
referring to the runtime arguments.
As the first stage is evaluated, operations on symbolic values become operations
in the generated #acr-long("ir").
The final #ir is the result of the first stage of evaluation.

The second stage involves compiling the #ir generated in the first stage of
evaluation into the native code for the target machine.
In the case of programming models targetting NVIDIA GPUs, this means the #ir is compiled
into PTX and then into the cubin format and then the kernel is launched using the CUDA driver api
// #footnote[Note that the driver api then JIT-compiles the PTX into SASS code!]
.

