CuTe DSL#footnote[As of version 4.8, which is the latest version at the time of writing.]
handles this with a third stage of evaluation prior to running the program
in the Python interpreter.
The three stages of the CuTe DSL compiler are _pre-staging_ where the AST is preprocessed,
the _meta stage_ where the Python interpreter evaluates the program generated by the first stage,
and the _object stage_, where the #ir generated by evaluating the program from the first stage
is compiled into the native object format and executed on the GPU.
Guray Ozen describes this in detail in his LLVM Dev conference talk @Ozen2025_CUTLASS_PythonDSL_CUTEDSL.

Note that this AST-transformation stage is similar to macro-expansion in Lisp (#xref(<sec:lisp>)),
except Python does not have macros, so DSL designers must use decorators to
perform #acr-short("ast")-level transformations themselves without the help of the language.

